---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Diagram from '../components/Diagram.astro';

const faq = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'FAQPage',
  mainEntity: [
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Is DNS TTL measured in seconds or minutes?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: "DNS TTL is always measured and stored in seconds inside DNS zone files. For example, a 5-minute TTL is written as 300, a 1-hour TTL is 3600, and a 24-hour TTL is 86400, even if your DNS provider's web dashboard displays a dropdown in minutes or hours.",
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What does "Auto" TTL mean in Cloudflare?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: "In Cloudflare DNS, setting a record's TTL to Auto assigns a default TTL of 300 seconds (5 minutes) for unproxied (DNS-only) records. For proxied (Orange Cloud) records, Cloudflare automatically manages edge caching and serves a short 300-second TTL to external resolvers.",
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'Does lowering my DNS TTL clear caches that already have the old record?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: "No, lowering a record's TTL only applies to new lookups fetched after you save the change. Any recursive resolver that cached your record earlier continues counting down the previous TTL timer until it expires naturally.",
      },
    },
    {
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: 'What is a good default TTL for A and MX records?',
      acceptedAnswer: {
        '@type': 'Answer',
        text: 'A TTL of 3600 seconds (1 hour) is the ideal everyday default for production A and AAAA web records, balancing fast cached lookups with 1-hour recovery if an IP changes. For stable MX mail records, 3600 to 14400 seconds (1 to 4 hours) is standard.',
      },
    },
  ],
};
---
<BaseLayout
  title="DNS TTL Explained: What Time to Live Means and Best Values in Seconds"
  description="DNS TTL explained: what Time to Live means, why it is measured in seconds, conversion chart (60s to 86400s), recommended values by record type, and SOA negative caching."
  schema={[faq]}
  breadcrumb={[["Home", "/"], ["DNS Guide", "/dns/"], ["TTL Explained"]]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/dns/" class="hover:text-accent">DNS Guide</a>
      <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">TTL Explained</span>
    </nav>

    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">DNS TTL Explained: What Time to Live Means and Best Values in Seconds</h1>

    <div class="pd-answer" data-vs="answer">
      <p class="text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">
        In DNS, Time to Live (TTL) is <strong class="text-ink">a numerical timer measured in seconds that tells recursive DNS resolvers how long to cache a record before requesting a fresh copy</strong>. Authoritative nameservers attach a TTL value (such as <code>3600</code> for 1 hour) to every DNS response. The length of a DNS TTL balances fast website lookup speeds against how quickly DNS changes take effect.
      </p>
    </div>

    <Diagram name="ttl-lifecycle" caption="The DNS cache lifecycle: local and recursive resolvers serve cached records until the TTL expires" />

    <nav class="pd-toc" aria-label="On this page">
      <p class="font-mono text-[10px] uppercase tracking-[0.18em] text-accent">On this page</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="#how-does-a-dns-ttl-countdown-timer-work-authoritative-vs-recursive-servers">How a DNS TTL countdown timer works</a></li>
        <li><a href="#common-dns-ttl-values-in-seconds-conversion-chart-and-use-cases">Common DNS TTL values in seconds</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-are-the-best-ttl-values-for-each-dns-record-type-a-cname-mx-txt-ns">Best TTL values by DNS record type</a></li>
        <li><a href="#high-ttl-vs-low-ttl-what-are-the-performance-cost-and-security-trade-offs">High TTL vs low TTL trade-offs</a></li>
        <li><a href="#how-does-soa-negative-cache-ttl-work-for-non-existent-domains-nxdomain">SOA negative cache TTL (NXDOMAIN)</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-is-the-difference-between-dns-ttl-ip-packet-ttl-and-cdn-cache-ttl">DNS TTL vs IP packet TTL vs CDN TTL</a></li>
        <li><a href="#frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ul>
    </nav>

    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 id="how-does-a-dns-ttl-countdown-timer-work-authoritative-vs-recursive-servers">How Does a DNS TTL Countdown Timer Work? (Authoritative vs Recursive Servers)</h2>
      <p>
        A DNS TTL timer works across <strong class="text-ink">5 sequential caching steps where your authoritative nameserver serves a fixed starting number of seconds and recursive resolvers count that number down to zero</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        The resolution and caching lifecycle progresses through these ordered stages:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Configure</strong> a static TTL value in seconds (such as <code>3600</code>) on a zone resource record.</li>
        <li><strong>Return</strong> the record and full <code>3600-second</code> TTL when a <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Recursive Resolver: a DNS server that queries authoritative servers on behalf of client devices and caches responses">recursive resolver</abbr> (like Cloudflare <code>1.1.1.1</code> or Google <code>8.8.8.8</code>) queries the domain.</li>
        <li><strong>Store</strong> the response in resolver RAM and decrement the countdown timer by 1 every second.</li>
        <li><strong>Answer</strong> subsequent queries directly from cache using the remaining countdown value (for instance, returning <code>2400</code> twenty minutes later) without querying the authoritative server.</li>
        <li><strong>Discard</strong> the cached record once the countdown reaches <code>0</code> and query your authoritative nameserver again on the next request.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Querying your <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Authoritative Nameserver: the definitive source server that holds and publishes the official DNS zone records for a domain">authoritative nameserver</abbr> always returns the full configured TTL (such as <code>3600</code>), whereas querying a public recursive resolver returns a ticking countdown number. Caching hops are explored in <a href="/how-dns-works/">How DNS Works</a>, and live resolver values can be audited via the <a href="/ttl-checker/">DNS TTL Checker</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="common-dns-ttl-values-in-seconds-conversion-chart-and-use-cases">Common DNS TTL Values in Seconds (Conversion Chart and Use Cases)</h2>
      <p>
        DNS zone files and control panels always express TTL values in <strong class="text-ink">raw seconds rather than minutes or hours</strong> (for example, <code>300</code> equals 5 minutes and <code>86400</code> equals 24 hours). The table below converts the 7 standard DNS TTL values from seconds into human time and defines when to use each setting.
      </p>

      <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
        <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
            <thead><tr><th>TTL Value (Seconds)</th><th>Equivalent Time</th><th>Category</th><th>Primary Use Case</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">60</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">1 minute</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Ultra-Low</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Active automated DNS failover, multi-CDN traffic steering, and live cutover testing.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">300</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">5 minutes</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Low (Pre-Migration)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Standard pre-migration setting lowered 24 to 48 hours before changing an IP or mail host.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">1800</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">30 minutes</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Moderate</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Frequently updated web applications, staging subdomains, and dynamic API endpoints.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">3600</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">1 hour</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim"><strong>Standard Default</strong></td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Recommended everyday balance for production A, AAAA, CNAME, and TXT records.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">14400</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">4 hours</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Medium-High</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Stable corporate websites and verified SPF or DMARC records that rarely change.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">43200</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">12 hours</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">High</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Long-standing MX mail routing records and DKIM public key records.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">86400</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">24 hours (1 day)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Maximum Standard</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Static NS delegations, CAA records, and infrastructure that never changes IP addresses.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-mono text-xs font-semibold text-accent">172800</td><td class="font-medium text-ink">48 hours (2 days)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Registry Parent Default</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Fixed TTL used by TLD registries (.com, .net) for parent NS and glue records.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p>
        Public recursive resolvers cap maximum cache lifetimes between 1 and 7 days (<code>86400</code> to <code>604800</code> seconds) regardless of larger values in zone files. Consumer ISP resolvers often enforce a minimum cache floor between <code>60</code> and <code>3600</code> seconds, ignoring ultra-short timers like <code>0</code> or <code>5</code> seconds to save bandwidth.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-are-the-best-ttl-values-for-each-dns-record-type-a-cname-mx-txt-ns">What Are the Best TTL Values for Each DNS Record Type? (A, CNAME, MX, TXT, NS)</h2>
      <p>
        Different DNS record types require <strong class="text-ink">different TTL settings based on how frequently their target values change and how critical they are to uptime</strong>. The table below lists the recommended production TTL and pre-migration TTL for the 7 primary DNS record types.
      </p>

      <section class="not-prose my-4" aria-label="Reference table">
        <div class="glass rounded-2xl p-5 shadow-lg sm:p-7 overflow-x-auto">
          <table class="pdns-table" style="width:100%">
            <thead><tr><th>Record Type</th><th>Normal Production TTL</th><th>Pre-Migration TTL</th><th>Why This TTL Range Works Best</th></tr></thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">A and AAAA</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">300 to 3600 s (5m - 1h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">60 to 300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Web server IPs change during migrations, scaling, or failover; shorter TTLs accelerate traffic switching.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">CNAME</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">1800 to 3600 s (30m - 1h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Alias targets rarely change, and the underlying canonical A record manages its own IP caching lifecycle.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">MX</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">3600 to 43200 s (1h - 12h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Mail server hosts change infrequently; higher TTLs preserve stable mail routing across SMTP relays.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">TXT (SPF, DMARC)</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">1800 to 3600 s (30m - 1h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">A 1-hour TTL permits rapid recovery if a new third-party email sending service requires policy inclusion.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">TXT (DKIM Keys)</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">3600 to 86400 s (1h - 24h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Public cryptographic keys remain static until scheduled rotation, making long caching safe and efficient.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">CAA</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">3600 to 86400 s (1h - 24h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">300 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Certificate Authority authorizations change only when switching SSL vendors, justifying longer TTLs.</td></tr>
              <tr><td class="font-medium text-ink">NS</td><td class="text-sm font-mono text-accent">86400 s (24h)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">3600 s</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Nameserver delegations remain static for years; parent TLD registries enforce 48 hours regardless of child values.</td></tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </section>

      <p>
        Dynamic routing designs like active-passive server failover depend directly on short <code>60-second</code> TTLs to reroute traffic within seconds, as detailed in <a href="/dns-load-balancing/">DNS Load Balancing</a> and the core protocol breakdown in <a href="/what-is-dns/">What Is DNS</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="high-ttl-vs-low-ttl-what-are-the-performance-cost-and-security-trade-offs">High TTL vs Low TTL: What Are the Performance, Cost, and Security Trade-Offs?</h2>
      <p>
        Choosing between a high TTL (<code>14400</code> to <code>86400</code> seconds) and a low TTL (<code>60</code> to <code>300</code> seconds) involves <strong class="text-ink">4 operational trade-offs across page-load latency, authoritative query costs, DDoS resilience, and migration agility</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        System administrators balance these 4 architectural considerations:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Visitor Resolution Latency:</strong> A <strong>High TTL</strong> keeps records cached at local ISP resolvers, returning warm responses in <code>1 to 15 ms</code>. A <strong>Low TTL</strong> forces resolvers to re-query authoritative nameservers, adding <code>40 to 150 ms</code> on cache misses.</li>
        <li><strong>Authoritative Query Volume and Costs:</strong> Managed DNS providers bill per million queries. Dropping your TTL from <code>3600</code> seconds to <code>60</code> seconds multiplies query volume and monthly DNS invoices by 10x to 30x.</li>
        <li><strong>DDoS Outage Resilience:</strong> A <strong>High TTL</strong> buffers websites against upstream DNS outages because resolvers serve cached answers for hours. A <code>60-second</code> TTL causes domains to go dark globally within one minute if nameservers become unreachable.</li>
        <li><strong>Migration and Rollback Agility:</strong> A <strong>Low TTL</strong> (<code>300</code> seconds) allows teams to cut over to new hosting infrastructure or revert broken deployments in 5 minutes rather than waiting 24 hours.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The proven operational strategy relies on running a standard <code>3600-second</code> (1-hour) TTL during normal periods, lowering it to <code>300</code> seconds 24 to 48 hours prior to maintenance, and restoring the 1-hour setting once verified. Planning cutovers is detailed in <a href="/change-dns-records-safely/">How to Change DNS Records Without Downtime</a> and <a href="/dns-propagation/">DNS Propagation</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="how-does-soa-negative-cache-ttl-work-for-non-existent-domains-nxdomain">How Does SOA Negative Cache TTL Work for Non-Existent Domains (NXDOMAIN)?</h2>
      <p>
        Caching for non-existent domain names (<abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="NXDOMAIN (Non-Existent Domain): a DNS response code indicating that the queried domain name does not exist">NXDOMAIN</abbr>) is governed by <strong class="text-ink">the <code>MINIMUM</code> negative cache timer at the end of your domain's <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Start of Authority (SOA): a foundational DNS record containing administrative parameters and zone transfer timers">Start of Authority (SOA)</abbr> record</strong> rather than a standard resource record TTL.
      </p>
      <p>
        Authoritative zone files format SOA timers according to this structure:
      </p>
      <pre class="rounded-lg border border-line bg-panel-2 p-4 font-mono text-xs text-accent-soft"><code>example.com. 3600 IN SOA ns1.example.com. admin.example.com. (
    2026102901 ; Serial number
    7200       ; Refresh (2 hours)
    3600       ; Retry (1 hour)
    1209600    ; Expire (14 days)
    3600       ; Negative Cache TTL / MINIMUM (1 hour)
)</code></pre>
      <p>
        Querying a non-existent subdomain (such as <code>staging.example.com</code>) before creating its DNS record causes recursive resolvers to cache the negative NXDOMAIN response according to <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="DNS Negative Caching: specifies caching requirements for negative NXDOMAIN and NODATA answers">RFC 2308</abbr> specifications. Creating the record minutes later will not make it resolve immediately on that device until the negative cache timer expires or the user flushes their system cache using <a href="/flush-dns-cache/">How to Flush DNS Cache</a>. Full timer parameters are documented in <a href="/soa-record/">SOA Record</a> and <a href="/soa-record-lookup/">SOA Record Lookup</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="what-is-the-difference-between-dns-ttl-ip-packet-ttl-and-cdn-cache-ttl">What Is the Difference Between DNS TTL, IP Packet TTL, and CDN Cache TTL?</h2>
      <p>
        The acronym TTL appears in <strong class="text-ink">3 separate networking contexts: DNS Record TTL (measured in seconds), IP Packet TTL (measured in router hops), and HTTP/CDN Cache TTL (measured in seconds)</strong>, each operating at distinct protocol layers.
      </p>
      <p>
        Network engineers distinguish these 3 implementations:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>DNS Record TTL (Seconds):</strong> Stored inside resource records to instruct recursive resolvers how long to cache IP mappings before re-querying authoritative servers.</li>
        <li><strong>IP Packet TTL / Hop Limit (Router Hops, 1 to 255):</strong> An 8-bit field in IPv4 headers (Hop Limit in IPv6) decremented at every routing hop to prevent infinite loops. The <code>ttl=56</code> metric in <code>ping</code> reflects remaining router hops, not DNS caching timers.</li>
        <li><strong>CDN and HTTP Cache-Control TTL (Seconds):</strong> Transmitted in HTTP headers (such as <code>Cache-Control: max-age=86400</code>) directing browsers and <abbr class="pd-term" data-tip="Anycast: a network addressing method that routes client queries to the topologically nearest server location">Anycast</abbr> CDN edges how long to store static files.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Inspecting a domain live TTL countdown is straightforward using terminal commands like <code>dig +noall +answer example.com A @1.1.1.1</code>, where the second column outputs the remaining seconds before cache expiration. Monitoring your domain DNS TTL values, maintaining a balanced 300 to 86400 seconds caching schedule, and executing timely pre-migration adjustments ensures authoritative nameservers and recursive resolver caches deliver optimal performance. Webmasters can audit multi-resolver caching via the <a href="/ttl-checker/">DNS TTL Checker</a> and monitor global record propagation through the <a href="/dns-propagation-checker/">DNS Propagation Checker</a>.
      </p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink" id="frequently-asked-questions">Frequently asked questions</h2>
    <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3">
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is DNS TTL measured in seconds or minutes?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">DNS TTL is <strong>always measured and stored in seconds</strong> inside DNS zone files. For example, a 5-minute TTL is written as <strong>300</strong>, a 1-hour TTL is <strong>3600</strong>, and a 24-hour TTL is <strong>86400</strong>, even if your DNS provider's web dashboard displays a dropdown in minutes or hours.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What does "Auto" TTL mean in Cloudflare?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">In Cloudflare DNS, setting a record's TTL to <strong>Auto</strong> assigns a default TTL of <strong>300 seconds (5 minutes)</strong> for unproxied (DNS-only) records. For proxied (Orange Cloud) records, Cloudflare automatically manages edge caching and serves a short 300-second TTL to external resolvers.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does lowering my DNS TTL clear caches that already have the old record?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">No, lowering a record's TTL <strong>only applies to new lookups fetched after you save the change</strong>. Any recursive resolver that cached your record earlier continues counting down the previous TTL timer until it expires naturally.</p>
      </details>
      <details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40">
        <summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is a good default TTL for A and MX records?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary>
        <p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A TTL of <strong>3600 seconds (1 hour)</strong> is the ideal everyday default for production <strong>A and AAAA</strong> web records, balancing fast cached lookups with 1-hour recovery if an IP changes. For stable <strong>MX</strong> mail records, <strong>3600 to 14400 seconds (1 to 4 hours)</strong> is standard.</p>
      </details>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
